<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>FlowX.Ai</RootNamespace>
    <Description>
      Reviews a FlowX manifest and reports what the document itself can prove. Reads
      flowx.manifest.json and nothing else — no repository, no telemetry, no database.
    </Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    No PackageReference and no ProjectReference, and both are the point rather than a
    coincidence of the current rule set.

    docs/13-AI-Native.md §5 promises the AI layer is removable: "uninstall FlowX.Ai and
    everything else works identically". A project that linked FlowX.Core would make that
    a claim about the uninstall order instead of a fact. And ADR-0005 says the manifest
    is self-describing; FlowX.Cli is the standing evidence, because CliLinksNoFlowXAssembly
    forbids it a project reference. This is the second consumer to take that shape, and it
    is the one that matters most: if reasoning about the architecture needed a FlowX type,
    no third-party reviewer could ever be written.

    System.Text.Json is in the shared framework, so reading the document costs no row in
    docs/DEPENDENCIES.md — the same argument samples/ecommerce makes for hand-writing its
    telemetry rather than referencing an SDK.
  -->

</Project>
